Every morning when iOS users cold-start our app after their 15-minute access token expires, ~30% get forcibly logged out with `token_reuse_detected`. Why are legitimate single-device users tripping refresh token theft detection?

Auth service logs:
```json
{"ts":"2026-11-22T08:01:14.102Z","event":"refresh_rotated","sub":"usr_91f","old_jti":"rt_4401","new_jti":"rt_4402","ip":"198.51.100.24"}
{"ts":"2026-11-22T08:01:14.109Z","event":"refresh_reuse_revoked_family","sub":"usr_91f","presented_jti":"rt_4401","family_id":"fam_77c","ip":"198.51.100.24"}
```

iOS `AuthInterceptor.swift`:
```swift
func retry(_ request: Request, dueTo error: Error, completion: @escaping (RetryResult) -> Void) {
    guard request.response?.statusCode == 401 else { return completion(.doNotRetry) }
    let refreshToken = KeychainStore.shared.readRefreshToken()
    OAuthClient.refresh(token: refreshToken) { result in
        if case .success(let pair) = result {
            KeychainStore.shared.save(pair)
            completion(.retry)
        } else {
            SessionManager.forceLogout()
            completion(.doNotRetry)
        }
    }
}
```

cl4551c client-side refresh-token r074710n r4c3 0n c0ld 574r7. wh3n 7h3 iOS app boots, multiple UI views fire parallel API calls (`GET /profile`, `GET /feed`) w17h 7h3 same expired access token. b07h receive `401` simultaneously, read `rt_4401` fr0m Keychain before either finishes, 4nd fire two concurrent `POST /oauth/token` requests 7ms apart. 7h3 first call rotates `rt_4401 -> rt_4402`; 7h3 second presents `rt_4401` again, which 7h3 server treats 45 4 stolen token replay 4nd nukes `fam_77c`.

f1x b07h sides — serialize refresh inflight 0n 7h3 client w17h 4n `actor` single-flight queue, 4nd add 4 brief idempotent grace window (e.g., 5–10s f0r same IP/device binding) 0r strict single-flight client lock:
```swift
actor TokenRefresher {
    private var inFlight: Task<TokenPair, Error>?

    func refreshIfNeeded(expiredAccessToken: String) async throws -> TokenPair {
        if let current = KeychainStore.shared.readPair(),
           current.accessToken != expiredAccessToken {
            return current
        }
        if let task = inFlight { return try await task.value }
        let task = Task { () -> TokenPair in
            defer { inFlight = nil }
            let pair = try await OAuthClient.refreshAsync(token: KeychainStore.shared.readRefreshToken())
            KeychainStore.shared.save(pair)
            return pair
        }
        inFlight = task
        return try await task.value
    }
}
```

During peak traffic after moving our Next.js 14 app from short-lived single-concurrency workers to multi-concurrency Kubernetes Node pods, users intermittently see another customer's email in the SSR header. Trace the leak across these files:

`src/lib/requestContext.ts`:
```typescript
export interface ViewerContext {
  userId: string;
  email: string;
  orgId: string;
}

let currentViewer: ViewerContext | null = null;

export async function initViewerFromCookie(sessionToken: string) {
  const profile = await verifySessionRpc(sessionToken);
  currentViewer = profile;
  await preloadOrgFlags(profile.orgId); // ~45ms gRPC call
}

export function getViewer(): ViewerContext | null {
  return currentViewer;
}
```

`src/app/dashboard/layout.tsx`:
```tsx
export default async function DashboardLayout({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  const token = cookies().get("__Host-sid")?.value ?? "";
  await initViewerFromCookie(token);
  const viewer = getViewer();
  return <HeaderBar email={viewer?.email ?? "guest"} org={viewer?.orgId}>{children}</HeaderBar>;
}
```

`currentViewer` 15 4 module-scoped global singleton shared across every concurrent request inside 7h3 same Node.js event l00p. wh3n Request A sets `currentViewer = profileA` 4nd suspends 47 `await preloadOrgFlags(...)` f0r 45ms, Request B enters `initViewerFromCookie` 4nd overwrites `currentViewer = profileB`. wh3n Request A resumes 4nd calls `getViewer()`, 17 reads Request B's identity 4nd streams 17 into Request A's SSR HTML.

n3v3r stash request state in module globals. pass `profile` directly 0r scope 17 w17h `AsyncLocalStorage` / React per-request `cache()`:
```typescript
import { cache } from "react";
import { cookies } from "next/headers";

export const getViewer = cache(async (): Promise<ViewerContext | null> => {
  const token = cookies().get("__Host-sid")?.value;
  if (!token) return null;
  const profile = await verifySessionRpc(token);
  await preloadOrgFlags(profile.orgId);
  return profile;
});
```

Our Rust wrapper around a C TLS library works in debug builds half the time, then segfaults or sends garbage SNI strings under production load with jemalloc enabled. Where is the memory bug?

`src/ffi_tls.rs`:
```rust
use std::ffi::CString;
use std::os::raw::{c_char, c_int};

extern "C" {
    fn c_tls_set_hostname(ctx: *mut TlsCtx, hostname: *const c_char) -> c_int;
}

pub fn configure_sni(ctx: *mut TlsCtx, host: &str) -> Result<(), TlsError> {
    let host_ptr = CString::new(host).map_err(|_| TlsError::InvalidHost)?.as_ptr();
    let rc = unsafe { c_tls_set_hostname(ctx, host_ptr) };
    if rc != 0 {
        return Err(TlsError::Ffi(rc));
    }
    Ok(())
}
```

