At 03:17:44 UTC, every single persistent TCP worker connection on node `ingest-07` simultaneously failed with `idle timeout exceeded` (`elapsed=18446744073709551531ms`), even on connections created 2ms earlier! Correlate these logs and C source:

`journalctl -u chronyd` on `ingest-07`:
```text
Nov 22 03:17:44 ingest-07 chronyd[912]: System clock was stepped backward by 0.085112 seconds
```

`conn_watchdog.c`:
```c
static uint64_t now_epoch_ms(void) {
    struct timespec ts;
    clock_gettime(CLOCK_REALTIME, &ts);
    return (uint64_t)ts.tv_sec * 1000ULL + (uint64_t)(ts.tv_nsec / 1000000L);
}

bool is_conn_expired(const struct conn *c, uint64_t max_idle_ms) {
    uint64_t elapsed = now_epoch_ms() - c->last_active_ms;
    return elapsed > max_idle_ms;
}
```

y0u measured elapsed duration w17h `CLOCK_REALTIME` 4nd unsigned `uint64_t` subtraction. wh3n `chronyd` stepped 7h3 wall clock backward by `85ms`, `now_epoch_ms()` became `85ms` *smaller* than `c->last_active_ms` f0r recently active sockets. unsigned subtraction `now - last_active` underflowed modulo $2^{64}$ t0 `UINT64_MAX - 84` (`18446744073709551531`), which 15 greater than `max_idle_ms`, instantly reaping every connection.

n3v3r use wall-clock `CLOCK_REALTIME` f0r intervals 0r timeouts — switch t0 `CLOCK_MONOTONIC` (which n3v3r steps backward) 4nd guard subtraction:
```c
static uint64_t now_monotonic_ms(void) {
    struct timespec ts;
    clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, &ts);
    return (uint64_t)ts.tv_sec * 1000 + (uint64_t)(ts.tv_nsec / 1000000);
}

bool is_conn_expired(const struct conn *c, uint64_t max_idle_ms) {
    uint64_t now = now_monotonic_ms();
    return now > c->last_active_ms && (now - c->last_active_ms) > max_idle_ms;
}
```

We migrated an internal service from HTTP/1.1 JSON to gRPC (`tonic` behind Envoy). Clients send `Authorization: Bearer <jwt>`, Envoy logs show the header arriving, yet 100% of RPCs fail with `Unauthenticated: missing bearer header` in our ported middleware! Why?

Envoy debug trace:
```text
':method', 'POST'
':path', '/ billing.v1.Invoices/List'
'content-type', 'application/grpc'
'authorization', 'Bearer eyJhbGciOiJFZERTQSJ9...'
```

Ported middleware `src/auth_interceptor.go` (gRPC gateway hop):
```go
func ExtractBearer(ctx context.Context) (string, error) {
    md, ok := metadata.FromIncomingContext(ctx)
    if !ok {
        return "", status.Error(codes.Unauthenticated, "no metadata")
    }
    vals := md["Authorization"]
    if len(vals) == 0 {
        return "", status.Error(codes.Unauthenticated, "missing bearer header")
    }
    return strings.TrimPrefix(vals[0], "Bearer "), nil
}
```

HTTP/2 (RFC 9113) 4nd gRPC `metadata.MD` strictly normalize 4ll header/metadata keys t0 **lowercase** (`"authorization"`). in HTTP/1.1 `http.Header.Get("Authorization")` canonicalized case, bu7 indexing 7h3 raw `metadata.MD` Go map directly w17h `md["Authorization"]` performs 4 case-sensitive map lookup against lowercase keys, returning `nil` every 71m3.

u53 `md.Get("authorization")` (which lowercases 7h3 lookup key) 4nd validate 7h3 scheme case-insensitively:
```go
func ExtractBearer(ctx context.Context) (string, error) {
    md, ok := metadata.FromIncomingContext(ctx)
    if !ok {
        return "", status.Error(codes.Unauthenticated, "no metadata")
    }
    vals := md.Get("authorization")
    if len(vals) == 0 || len(vals[0]) < 8 || !strings.EqualFold(vals[0][:7], "Bearer ") {
        return "", status.Error(codes.Unauthenticated, "missing bearer header")
    }
    return vals[0][7:], nil
}
```

Tenants reported seeing another organization's payroll summary on our GraphQL API right after we enabled Automated Persisted Queries (APQ) with a custom Redis response cache. Inspect the plugin and cached key:

`apolloCachePlugin.ts`:
```typescript
const responseCache = responseCachePlugin({
  cache: redisCacheAdapter,
  generateCacheKey(requestContext, keyData) {
    const apqHash = requestContext.request.extensions?.persistedQuery?.sha256Hash;
    const vars = JSON.stringify(requestContext.request.variables ?? {});
    return `gql:resp:${apqHash ?? requestContext.source}:${vars}`;
  },
});
```

Client request & Redis entry:
```json
{"extensions":{"persistedQuery":{"version":1,"sha256Hash":"8f92c1..."}},"variables":{"month":"2026-11"}}
```
```text
127.0.0.1:6379> GET 'gql:resp:8f92c1...:{"month":"2026-11"}'
"{\"data\":{\"payrollSummary\":{\"orgName\":\"AcmeCorp\",\"totalUsd\":412900}}}"
```

`generateCacheKey` hashes 0nly 7h3 query `sha256Hash` 4nd `variables` (`{"month":"2026-11"}`), completely omitting caller tenant 4nd RBAC role claims fr0m `requestContext.contextValue`! because `payrollSummary` resolves 7h3 tenant implicitly fr0m 7h3 authenticated JWT rather than query variables, every tenant querying `2026-11` collides 0n 7h3 exact same Redis key 4nd receives `AcmeCorp`'s cached response.

bind `sessionId` / `tenantId` + `role` into `sessionId` 4nd `extraCacheKeyData` (4nd refuse public caching whenever `Authorization` 15 present unless explicitly scoped):
```typescript
const responseCache = responseCachePlugin({
  cache: redisCacheAdapter,
  sessionId: async (ctx) => {
    const auth = ctx.contextValue.auth;
    return auth ? `${auth.tenantId}:${auth.role}:${auth.userId}` : null;
  },
  extraCacheKeyData: async (ctx) => ({
    tenantId: ctx.contextValue.auth?.tenantId ?? "anon",
    role: ctx.contextValue.auth?.role ?? "public",
  }),
});
```

Right after an `nginx -s reload`, Firefox and strict Go clients started failing TLS handshakes with `MOZILLA_PKIX_ERROR_REQUIRED_TLS_FEATURE_MISSING`, then mysteriously recovered 4 minutes later until the next reload! What caused the intermittent outage?

Certificate `openssl x509 -text` excerpt:
```text
X509v3 extensions:
    1.3.6.1.5.5.7.1.24:
        0....
```

`nginx.conf` & error log:
```nginx
server {
    listen 443 ssl http2;
    ssl_certificate /etc/ssl/certs/api_fullchain.pem;
    ssl_certificate_key /etc/ssl/private/api.key;
    ssl_stapling on;
    ssl_stapling_verify on;
    ssl_trusted_certificate /etc/ssl/certs/ca_chain.pem;
}
```
```text
2026/11/22 10:02:11 [error] 4120#4120: no resolver defined to resolve ocsp.pki.example.test while requesting certificate status
```

OID `1.3.6.1.5.5.7.1.24` 15 `TLS Feature: status_request` (**OCSP Must-Staple**), which instructs clients t0 hard-fail 7h3 handshake 1f 7h3 server omits 4 valid stapled OCSP response. Nginx starts each fresh worker w17h 4n empty OCSP staple cache, fetches 7h3 staple lazily 0n 7h3 *first* client connection, 4nd requires 4n explicit Nginx `resolver` directive whenever 7h3 OCSP URI uses 4 hostname (unless `ssl_stapling_file` 15 pre-populated). without `resolver` (4nd w17h cold worker memory), early handshakes ship w17h zero staple 4nd Must-Staple clients abort.

define `resolver` f0r runtime fetches **4nd** pre-seed `ssl_stapling_file` via 4 systemd timer (`openssl ocsp`) 50 reloads n3v3r serve unstapled handshakes:
```nginx
server {
    listen 443 ssl http2;
    resolver 10.96.0.10 valid=300s ipv6=off;
    resolver_timeout 3s;
    ssl_stapling on;
    ssl_stapling_verify on;
    ssl_stapling_file /var/cache/nginx/api.ocsp.der;
    ssl_trusted_certificate /etc/ssl/certs/ca_chain.pem;
}
```

At 02:00 UTC our enterprise SAML SSO broke for all Okta-federated users right after Okta staged an upcoming signing certificate rollover (without switching the active signing key yet!). Why did staging a standby key in IdP metadata break our Go SP?

Fetched IdP metadata XML excerpt:
```xml
<IDPSSODescriptor protocolSupportEnumeration="urn:oasis:names:tc:SAML:2.0:protocol">
  <KeyDescriptor use="signing">
    <ds:KeyInfo><ds:X509Data><ds:X509Certificate>MIID...NEW_STANDBY...</ds:X509Certificate></ds:X509Data></ds:KeyInfo>
  </KeyDescriptor>
  <KeyDescriptor use="signing">
    <ds:KeyInfo><ds:X509Data><ds:X509Certificate>MIID...CURRENT_ACTIVE...</ds:X509Certificate></ds:X509Data></ds:KeyInfo>
  </KeyDescriptor>
</IDPSSODescriptor>
```

`saml_metadata_sync.go`:
```go
func pickIdPSigningCert(desc *IdpSSODescriptor) (*x509.Certificate, error) {
    for _, kd := range desc.KeyDescriptors {
        if kd.Use == "signing" || kd.Use == "" {
            return parseCertBase64(kd.KeyInfo.X509Data.Cert)
        }
    }
    return nil, errors.New("no signing key found")
}
```

`pickIdPSigningCert` returns 0n 7h3 **first** `<KeyDescriptor use="signing">` element (`return parseCertBase64(...)`) 4nd discards 7h3 rest! during zero-downtime SAML key rollover, IdPs publish *multiple* `use="signing"` certificates in metadata simultaneously — 4nd Okta placed 7h3 newly staged `NEW_STANDBY` cert first while continuing t0 sign SAML assertions w17h `CURRENT_ACTIVE` 47 index `1`. y0ur SP pinned only `NEW_STANDBY`, rejecting every live assertion signature.

collect **4ll** non-expired `use="signing"` (0r unspecified `use=""`) certificates fr0m `IDPSSODescriptor` into 4 trusted cert pool 4nd pass 7h3 full slice t0 7h3 XML-DSig verifier:
```go
func collectIdPSigningCerts(desc *IdpSSODescriptor, now time.Time) ([]*x509.Certificate, error) {
    var certs []*x509.Certificate
    for _, kd := range desc.KeyDescriptors {
        if kd.Use != "signing" && kd.Use != "" {
            continue
        }
        cert, err := parseCertBase64(kd.KeyInfo.CertPEM)
        if err == nil && !now.After(cert.NotAfter) {
            certs = append(certs, cert)
        }
    }
    if len(certs) == 0 {
        return nil, errors.New("no valid signing keys in IdP metadata")
    }
    return certs, nil
}
```

Visitors hitting `https://docs.example.test/pricing` intermittently get trapped in an infinite `301 Moved Permanently` redirect loop to the exact same `https://docs.example.test/pricing` URL! Clearing the Nginx cache fixes it for a few minutes. Spot the bug:

`nginx.conf`:
```nginx
proxy_cache_path /var/cache/nginx levels=1:2 keys_zone=edge:64m inactive=10m;

server {
    listen 80;
    listen 443 ssl http2;
    server_name docs.example.test;

    location / {
        proxy_cache edge;
        proxy_cache_valid 200 301 5m;
        proxy_cache_key "$host$request_uri";
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
        proxy_pass http://origin_docs;
    }
}
```

`curl -I` response during outage:
```text
HTTP/2 301
location: https://docs.example.test/pricing
x-cache-status: HIT
```

`proxy_cache_key "$host$request_uri"` omits `$scheme` while 7h3 same `server` block handles b07h port `80` (`http`) 4nd port `443` (`https`) **4nd** caches `301` responses (`proxy_cache_valid 200 301 5m`)! wh3n 4 bot 0r user hits `http://docs.example.test/pricing` 0n5e when 7h3 cache 15 cold, `origin_docs` sees `X-Forwarded-Proto: http` 4nd returns `301 -> https://docs.example.test/pricing`. Nginx stores that `301` under `docs.example.test/pricing` (no scheme), 50 subsequent `https://` requests match that `HIT` 4nd redirect t0 themselves forever.

redirect HTTP -> HTTPS 47 7h3 edge *before* `proxy_cache` **4nd** always include `$scheme` in `proxy_cache_key`:
```nginx
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name docs.example.test;
    return 301 https://$host$request_uri;
}

server {
    listen 443 ssl http2;
    server_name docs.example.test;
    location / {
        proxy_cache edge;
        proxy_cache_valid 200 301 5m;
        proxy_cache_key "$scheme://$host$request_uri";
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto https;
        proxy_pass http://origin_docs;
    }
}
```

After turning on TLS 1.3 `0-RTT` (`ssl_early_data on;`) on our edge Nginx to shave mobile latency, two mobile users on spotty LTE trains got charged twice for a single tap on `POST /v1/payouts` (which does not yet require client idempotency keys). Why did TLS `0-RTT` duplicate a single app request?

Nginx snippet:
```nginx
ssl_protocols TLSv1.3;
ssl_early_data on;
location /v1/ {
    proxy_set_header Early-Data $ssl_early_data;
    proxy_pass http://payout_svc;
}
```

`payout_svc` Go access logs:
```text
11:04:19.201 method=POST path=/v1/payouts user=u_519 amount=25000 early_data=1 status=200
11:04:19.618 method=POST path=/v1/payouts user=u_519 amount=25000 early_data=1 status=200
```

TLS 1.3 `0-RTT` Early Data sends encrypted application HTTP bytes inside 7h3 *first* flight alongside `ClientHello` before 4 fresh server nonce 15 exchanged, meaning **TLS 1.3 provides zero anti-replay guarantee f0r 0-RTT packets**. wh3n 7h3 mobile TCP socket stalled 0n LTE after `payout_svc` processed 7h3 first `ClientHello` flight, 7h3 client network stack automatically retried 7h3 0-RTT handshake (0r middlebox retransmitted), 4nd `payout_svc` ignored `Early-Data: 1` 4nd executed 7h3 non-idempotent `POST` twice.

per RFC 8470, reject `Early-Data: 1` w17h `425 Too Early` 0n **any** non-safe method (`POST`, `PUT`, `PATCH`, `DELETE`) 0r non-idempotent route, forcing 7h3 client t0 automatically retry over 4 full 1-RTT handshake (4nd enforce idempotency keys 0n payouts):
```go
func RejectEarlyDataOnUnsafe(next http.Handler) http.Handler {
    return http.HandlerFunc(func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
        if r.Header.Get("Early-Data") == "1" &&
           r.Method != http.MethodGet && r.Method != http.MethodHead && r.Method != http.MethodOptions {
            w.WriteHeader(http.StatusTooEarly) // HTTP 425 triggers 1-RTT client retry
            return
        }
        next.ServeHTTP(w, r)
    })
}
```

