[Artifact 1: TLS Connection Setup (`src/tls_conn.c`)]
```c
SSL *accept_tls_client(SSL_CTX *ctx, int client_fd) {
    BIO *bio = BIO_new_socket(client_fd, BIO_NOCLOSE);
    SSL *ssl = SSL_new(ctx);
    if (!ssl || !bio) {
        SSL_free(ssl);
        BIO_free_all(bio);
        return NULL;
    }
    SSL_set_bio(ssl, bio, bio);
    if (SSL_accept(ssl) <= 0) {
        SSL_free(ssl);
        BIO_free_all(bio); // <-- Clean up bio on failed handshake?
        return NULL;
    }
    return ssl;
}
```

[Artifact 2: `glibc` Abort & Backtrace]
```
free(): double free detected in tcache 2
Program received signal SIGABRT, Aborted.
#3  CRYPTO_free () from libcrypto.so.3
#4  BIO_free () from libcrypto.so.3
#5  BIO_free_all () from libcrypto.so.3
#6  accept_tls_client () at src/tls_conn.c:13
```

[Artifact 3: OpenSSL `SSL_set_bio(3)` Manual Page]
```
SSL_set_bio() connects the BIOs rbio and wbio for the read and write operations of the TLS/SSL (encrypted) side of ssl.
This transfers ownership of rbio and wbio to ssl; they will be automatically freed by SSL_free().
```

Connect the dots: why did `accept_tls_client` crash only after `SSL_set_bio`, and how do we fix the cleanup paths?

**c4u54l c45c4d3:** `SSL_set_bio(ssl, bio, bio)` **transfers ownership** of `bio` irreversibly to `ssl` (4r71f4c7 3; passing the same `bio` for both `rbio` and `wbio` keeps refcount = 1 owned by `ssl`). When `SSL_accept(ssl)` failed on a truncated handshake (4r71f4c7 1), `SSL_free(ssl)` **already freed `bio`**. Calling `BIO_free_all(bio)` on the very next line freed that exact same heap chunk a second time, tripping `glibc`'s `tcache` double-free detector (`SIGABRT` in 4r71f4c7 2)!

